sentences.t:
Dixo el Çid, [el que en buen ora nasco]:
“graçias don abbat, e so vuestro pagado;
yo adobaré conducho pora mí e pora mi[o]s vasallos;

sentences.l:
Said the Çid, [the-one who in good hour was-born]:
“thanks lord abbot, and I-am your satisfied-one;
I shall-prepare provisions for me and for my vassals;

sentences.t:
Dues fijas dexo niñas e prendetlas en los braços;
aquí vos [las] acomiendo a vos, abbat don Sancho;
dellas e de mi mugier fagades todo recabdo.

sentences.l:
Two daughters I-leave little-girls and take-them in the arms;
here to-you [them] I-commend to you, abbot don Sancho;
of-them and of my wife may-you-take all care.

sentences.t:
Si essa despenssa vos falleçiere o vos menguare algo,
bien las abastad, yo assí vos lo mando;
por un marco que despendades al monesterio daré yo quatro.”

sentences.l:
If that provision to-you should-run-short or to-you should-lack anything,
well them provide, I thus to-you it command;
for one mark that you-may-spend on-the monastery I-will-give I four.”

sentences.t:
Por Castiella o[d]iendo van los pregones,
commo se va de tierra mio Çid el Canpeador;
unos dexan casas e otros onores.

sentences.l:
Through Castile hearing go the proclamations,
how himself goes from land my Çid the Campeador;
some leave houses and others honours.

sentences.t:
En aqués día a la puent de Arla[n]çón
çiento quinze cavalleros todos juntados son;
todos demandan por mio Çid el Campeador;

sentences.l:
On that day at the bridge of Arlançón
hundred fifteen knights all gathered are;
all ask for my Çid the Campeador;
